[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1735","stub":{"id":"1735","section":"Jacks River to U.S. 411 Bridge","altname":"","river":"Conasauga River","difficulty":"IItoIII","states":["USA-GEO","USA-TNN"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"3.27","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"levelFT","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"","id":"109118","location":{"latitude":"34.988","longitude":"-84.633"},"name":"Put In at Jacks River Trail trail head","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":"","id":"114353","location":{"latitude":"35.009822","longitude":"-84.734053"},"name":"Take Out at US 411","distance":"10.08","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[{"id":"iOcAoYpESXhKpZlVUhFF9","ext":"jpg","post_id":"AAXDaOuq20HdGDUVMCDZv","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Robin Chapman","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-08-24T23:54:18.000Z","updated_at":"2025-08-24T23:54:18.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/iOcAoYpESXhKpZlVUhFF9","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/iOcAoYpESXhKpZlVUhFF9"}},{"id":"CljrmcXGKCqrrUhQDlNso","ext":"jpg","post_id":"AAXDaOuq20HdGDUVMCDZv","description":"<p>We read that the river was rocky at 5.5 feet, but we put on at 2.94 anyway. If you are not looking for an adrenaline rush, you might enjoy this river at 2.94, although 3 feet might be more comfortable. If you can read the water and manuever your boat with accuracy, you will only have to drag your boat twice for about 10 feet each time. Everything else we managed to run. It took us about 4 ours to do the 6.3 mile run, but we loved it. It was technical and required that we pay attention to the river, but it was also gentle and beautiful from beginning to end. I would recommend 3 feet minimum, but you certainly don't need 6 feet to make the run!</p>","caption":"Conasauga from Snorkeling hole to Ladd Springs Rd","poi_name":null,"subject":"Can be run at 2.94","author":"Robin Chapman","poi_id":"109118","photo_date":"2025-08-24T23:54:18.000Z","updated_at":"2025-08-24T23:54:19.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/CljrmcXGKCqrrUhQDlNso","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/CljrmcXGKCqrrUhQDlNso"}}],"detail":{"id":"1735","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"Put-in: Jacks River Trail parking lot located on the Jacks River 0.2 miles upstream of the Jacks/Conasauga confluence and immediately upstream of the FS bridge.\n\nput-in: 34.9878, -84.63326\n\nput-in elevation: 949'\n\nTake out: Mile 10.1 (US 411): the State of Tennessee owns 100' of river-right streambank immediately downstream of US 411.\n\ntake out: 35.0099, -84.7340\n\ntake out elevation: 761'\n\nThe following intermediate access points have been used in the past and may or may not afford access presently.\n\n#1) at Mile 6.3 (as in, 6.3 miles from the put-in): on undeveloped privately-owned land that appears to get a healthy amount of respectful trespass.  Access here is appealing because the corridor transitions here from USFS forest to private, developed land.  Also because the gradient peters out.\n\nm6.3 take-out lat: 35.0044\n\nm6.3 take-out long: -84.6940\n\nm6.3 take-out elevation: 787'\n\nThere is a one-tenth-mile-long trail from the road to the m6.3 take-out. Limited shoulder parking and trailhead are located at lat/long: 35.0062,-84.6943.\n\n#2) at Mile 9.1: where Ball Play Rd snugs up against the river.\n\nm9.1 take-out lat: 35.01077\n\nm9.1 take-out long: -84.72195\n\nm9.1 take-out elevation: 768'\n\nOVERALL DESCRIPTION OF THE RUN\n\nBeautiful class II run through the Cherokee National Forest with exceptionally clean water draining out of the Cohutta Wilderness.\n\nLooking at the gradient by mile \\[fpm: 38,34,30,27,23,20,16,12,9,7\\] you can see how it starts out with a bang and progressively flattens along the way.\n\nFor the first six miles, the river runs through undeveloped National Forest with an average gradient of 28fpm.  There are two significant rapids in the first mile and a half that merit a II+ rating for their overall drop and fairly technical maneuvering (or, rock banging...take your pick).  More Class I-II rapids occur frequently throughout the first six miles. Below that, the remaining four miles are comparatively flat (~10fpm) and populated with houses and farms.\n\nFLOWS\n\nLike its big sister, the Upper Conasauga, this section doesn't run often. USGS 2384500 'CONASAUGA RIVER AT GA 286, NEAR ETON, GA' is the primary gauge but it leaves a lot to be desired.  It's located 24 miles downstream and lags this run by nearly half a day.  A sustained level of 5' is minimum.  6'--if it's sustained, not flashing--is good.  Typically, the Eton gauge has spiked to 12' or more prior to a day or two of boatable flows.\n\nThe bridge gauge (located 34.9746, 84.6449, [content/Photo/detail/photoid/9397](content/Photo/detail/photoid/9397)) one mile upstream of the put-in does a good job of indicating flows in the run. 0'4' is comfortably above minimum.  Also, just as with the Upper Conasauga, two other telemetry gauges in the watershed (2384540 USGS Mill Ck, 2385800 USGS Holly near Chatsworth) are important for judging when to go and when to stay home.  I look for Mill Ck to exceed 40cfs and Holly to exceed 100cfs.","averageGradient":"28","maxGradient":"38","length":"10.1","correlations":[{"reachID":"1735","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"levelFT","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"6","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"999"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"CONASAUGA RIVER AT GA 286, NEAR ETON, GA","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"02384500","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"215","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"3.27","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"34.8275","longitude":"-84.8509722"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/02384500","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/02384500/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":"\t\tGauge is located 24 miles downstream and therefore suffers a 12 hour lag.\r\nAlso look for 100+ cfs on Georgia's nearby Holly Creek gauge.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n","status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"3.27","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"levelFT","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["109118","114353"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-84.633233,34.9876],[-84.633712,34.98788],[-84.633917,34.988012],[-84.634116,34.988148],[-84.63418,34.988196],[-84.634271,34.988272],[-84.634299,34.9883],[-84.634459,34.988512],[-84.634686,34.988723],[-84.634717,34.988748],[-84.634772,34.988802],[-84.634833,34.988852],[-84.634975,34.988984],[-84.63506,34.989064],[-84.635111,34.989121],[-84.635226,34.989225],[-84.635322,34.989324],[-84.635353,34.989349],[-84.635437,34.98943],[-84.635487,34.989486],[-84.635573,34.989565],[-84.635604,34.98959],[-84.635656,34.989645],[-84.635741,34.989724],[-84.635772,34.989749],[-84.63594,34.989863],[-84.635979,34.989879],[-84.636021,34.98989],[-84.636096,34.989899],[-84.636235,34.99008],[-84.63626,34.990109],[-84.636681,34.990474],[-84.637296,34.99098],[-84.637423,34.991078],[-84.637459,34.991099],[-84.637523,34.991147],[-84.63759,34.991192],[-84.637662,34.991232],[-84.6378